qs.append({"q":"The outcome of the First Round Table Conference included general agreement that India would develop into a federation, though the conference achieved little because:","o":["The Indian National Congress, the primary nationalist voice, was absent, and civil disobedience continued in India","All parties reached full consensus immediately","The princely states refused any form of federal participation","Britain granted immediate dominion status, making further talks unnecessary"],"a":0,"e":"With the Congress boycotting the conference and civil disobedience ongoing, the British government realised any lasting constitutional settlement required Congress participation."})

qs.append({"q":"The Second Round Table Conference (September-December 1931) saw the Indian National Congress nominate which leader as its sole representative?","o":["Mahatma Gandhi","Motilal Nehru","Jawaharlal Nehru","Madan Mohan Malaviya"],"a":0,"e":"Gandhi attended as the Congress's sole official representative at the Second Round Table Conference, following the Gandhi-Irwin Pact."})

qs.append({"q":"At the Second Round Table Conference, Gandhi controversially argued that untouchables should NOT be treated as a separate minority because:","o":["He considered them to be Hindus, and thus part of the Hindu community rather than a distinct minority","He believed all minorities should be abolished entirely","He supported the Muslim League's position on separate electorates","He wanted to avoid discussing caste altogether"],"a":0,"e":"Gandhi insisted the depressed classes were Hindus and should not be granted separate electorates as a distinct minority, a position that clashed directly with Ambedkar's stance."})

qs.append({"q":"The Second Round Table Conference became deadlocked primarily over the question of:","o":["Minority representation, with Muslims, depressed classes, Christians and Anglo-Indians uniting in a 'Minorities' Pact' demanding separate electorates","The location of the next conference","The exact wording of the dominion status declaration","The role of the princely states in a future federation"],"a":0,"e":"The 'Minorities' Pact', uniting various communities behind demands for separate electorates, created an impasse Gandhi fiercely opposed, ultimately dooming the conference's progress."})

