qs.append({"q":"Which of the following provisions were part of the Communal Award?\n1. Separate electorates for depressed classes for a period of 20 years.\n2. A 'double vote' for depressed classes — one in separate electorates and one in general electorates.\n3. Reservation of 3 per cent seats for women in all provinces except the NWFP.\n4. Complete abolition of communal seat allocation in provincial legislatures.\nHow many of the above are correctly listed as provisions of the Communal Award?","o":["Only three","All four","Only two","Only one"],"a":0,"e":"Statements 1, 2 and 3 correctly describe the Communal Award's provisions; the award actually distributed seats on a communal basis in provincial legislatures rather than abolishing such allocation."})

qs.append({"q":"Gandhi's principal objection to the Communal Award's treatment of the depressed classes was that:","o":["Treating them as a separate political entity would undermine the campaign to abolish untouchability, keeping untouchables 'untouchable in perpetuity'","He believed the depressed classes deserved no representation at all","He supported separate electorates for all minorities except the depressed classes","He wanted the depressed classes to have fewer reserved seats"],"a":0,"e":"Gandhi feared that formalising the depressed classes as a distinct political minority via separate electorates would entrench untouchability rather than help eliminate it, undermining the deeper moral goal of its abolition."})

qs.append({"q":"To protest the Communal Award's provision for the depressed classes, Gandhi undertook an indefinite fast beginning:","o":["September 20, 1932","August 16, 1932","February 14, 1931","March 12, 1930"],"a":0,"e":"Gandhi began his indefinite fast on September 20, 1932, pressuring for a compromise on separate electorates for the depressed classes, which culminated in the Poona Pact."})

qs.append({"q":"The Poona Pact, signed by B.R. Ambedkar on behalf of the depressed classes on September 24, 1932, resulted in:","o":["Abandonment of separate electorates for depressed classes, but an increase in reserved seats from 71 to 147 in provincial legislatures","Continuation of separate electorates with fewer reserved seats","Complete removal of any reserved seats for depressed classes","Extension of separate electorates to all Hindu sub-castes"],"a":0,"e":"The Poona Pact replaced separate electorates with joint electorates while substantially increasing reserved seats for depressed classes — from 71 to 147 in provincial legislatures and 18% in the Central Legislature."})

qs.append({"q":"Which of the following is identified in the chapter as a negative long-term impact of the Poona Pact on the depressed classes?","o":["It made depressed class representatives vulnerable to being chosen as compliant 'stooges' by caste Hindu organisations under joint electorates","It granted complete political independence to the depressed classes from caste Hindu influence","It eliminated the need for any future reservation policy","It gave depressed classes a separate, sovereign political existence"],"a":0,"e":"Critics argue the joint electorate system under the Poona Pact allowed caste Hindu organisations to effectively select depressed-class representatives sympathetic to their interests, undermining genuine independent leadership for Dalits."})

qs.append({"q":"Following the Poona Pact, Gandhi launched a campaign against untouchability, establishing the All India Anti-Untouchability League in September 1932 and starting which weekly publication in January 1933?","o":["Harijan","Young India","Navjivan","Indian Opinion"],"a":0,"e":"Gandhi started the weekly Harijan in January 1933 as part of his intensified campaign against untouchability."})

qs.append({"q":"Gandhi's Harijan tour, undertaken from November 1933 to July 1934, covered approximately how many kilometres while collecting funds for the Harijan Sevak Sangh?","o":["20,000 km","5,000 km","50,000 km","1,000 km"],"a":0,"e":"Gandhi's extensive Harijan tour covered about 20,000 km, spreading his anti-untouchability message and raising funds for the Harijan Sevak Sangh."})

qs.append({"q":"Gandhi's approach to caste and untouchability differed from Ambedkar's in that Gandhi:","o":["Distinguished between abolishing untouchability and abolishing the caste system itself, whereas Ambedkar sought annihilation of the caste system entirely","Also sought complete annihilation of the caste system, agreeing fully with Ambedkar","Believed untouchability was sanctioned by scripture and should be preserved","Refused to engage with the issue of untouchability at all"],"a":0,"e":"Gandhi held that untouchability, not the caste/varna system itself, was the moral evil to be eradicated, believing varna could function without hierarchy; Ambedkar, by contrast, saw caste itself as the root problem requiring annihilation."})

qs.append({"q":"Regarding methods of political and social change, which of the following correctly distinguishes Gandhi's and Ambedkar's approaches?","o":["Gandhi relied on persuasion, morality and civil disobedience against unjust laws, while Ambedkar favoured legal and constitutional methods within the framework of law","Gandhi favoured strict legalism while Ambedkar favoured mass civil disobedience","Both leaders relied exclusively on parliamentary methods","Both leaders rejected the use of law entirely in favour of direct action"],"a":0,"e":"Gandhi's method centred on moral appeal, satyagraha and civil disobedience to unjust laws, while Ambedkar was more inclined towards working within legal and constitutional frameworks to secure rights."})

qs.append({"q":"Ambedkar denounced Gandhi's renaming of the Depressed Classes League as the 'Harijan Sevak Sangh' and subsequently left the organisation because he believed:","o":["For Gandhi, removal of untouchability was only a platform, not a sincere programme","Gandhi had fully adopted Ambedkar's programme for annihilation of caste","The new name accurately reflected genuine social transformation","The organisation had become irrelevant to Dalit concerns"],"a":0,"e":"Ambedkar was sceptical that Gandhi's 'Harijan' framing represented genuine structural change, viewing it as more symbolic gesture than substantive commitment to Dalit emancipation."})

qs.append({"q":"Regarding views on democracy and governance, which best captures the Gandhi-Ambedkar difference?","o":["Ambedkar favoured a parliamentary system and mass democracy as pressure on government, while Gandhi had limited respect for parliamentary systems, fearing mass democracy could lead to domination by leaders","Both leaders were equally enthusiastic supporters of parliamentary democracy","Gandhi favoured strong centralised parliamentary rule, while Ambedkar opposed all forms of representative government","Neither leader expressed any views on the nature of democratic governance"],"a":0,"e":"Ambedkar advocated a parliamentary system and believed mass democracy could empower the oppressed, whereas Gandhi was more sceptical of parliamentary structures, favouring decentralised self-governance and fearing leader-driven mass politics."})

qs.append({"q":"Regarding the relationship between religion and untouchability, Ambedkar's position (in his later years) differed from Gandhi's in that Ambedkar:","o":["Ultimately denounced Hinduism as unreformable and later left it, while Gandhi maintained that caste had nothing inherently to do with Hindu religious precepts","Believed Hinduism required no reform whatsoever","Agreed entirely with Gandhi that untouchability was unrelated to Hindu scripture","Refused to discuss the relationship between religion and caste at all"],"a":0,"e":"Ambedkar eventually concluded that Hinduism itself sanctioned caste and untouchability and could not be reformed from within, ultimately renouncing it, while Gandhi maintained caste was a 'perversive degeneration' separate from core religious teaching."})

qs.append({"q":"Justice Krishna Iyer's assessment of Gandhi and Ambedkar, quoted in the chapter, suggests that:","o":["Both shared a common opposition to social evil and imperial injustice, though a fundamental difference — more apparent than real — separated their approaches","The two leaders had no common ground whatsoever","Ambedkar was entirely dependent on Gandhi's guidance","Gandhi fully endorsed all of Ambedkar's constitutional proposals"],"a":0,"e":"Justice Krishna Iyer's assessment captures the nuanced relationship between the two leaders — shared moral concerns about injustice, alongside genuine but perhaps overstated ideological differences."})

